<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:localization="clr-namespace:IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Core.Localization;assembly=IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Core">

    <!--The Maptor half of an application's resources, in one dictionary.

        A host that shows Maptor WPF views merges the MahApps baseline and then this file:

            <Application.Resources>
                <ResourceDictionary>
                    <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/MahApps.Metro;component/Styles/Controls.xaml"/>
                        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/MahApps.Metro;component/Styles/Fonts.xaml"/>
                        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/MahApps.Metro;component/Styles/Themes/Light.Amber.xaml"/>
                        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Wpf;component/Assets/Maptor.All.xaml"/>
                    </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                </ResourceDictionary>
            </Application.Resources>

        MahApps' Styles/Controls.xaml is its own bundle of every per control dictionary, so the
        individual Controls.Slider / Controls.RadioButton / Controls.TabControl merges that some
        applications still carry are redundant. Any accent and mode may be used in place of
        Light.Amber, and ThemeHelper.ApplyTheme overrides the choice at runtime either way.

        WHY THE MAHAPPS LINES ARE NOT INSIDE THIS FILE. Several Maptor styles are BasedOn a
        MahApps key: IRI.Maptor.Styles.ButtonBase on MahApps.Styles.Button.Flat,
        IRI.Maptor.Styles.PasswordBoxBase on MahApps.Styles.PasswordBox, and likewise the Slider,
        ToggleSwitch and TextBox styles. A StaticResource inside a nested dictionary can reach
        Application.Resources and the dictionaries merged directly there, but NOT a sibling that
        is itself nested. Measured with the probe described in the design system README section 8:
        with the three MahApps merges moved into this file,
        IRI.Maptor.Styles.Button.Primary.Large throws "Cannot find resource named
        MahApps.Styles.Button.Flat" the first time it is used, even though that same key resolves
        fine through Application.TryFindResource in the same run. So the MahApps baseline has to
        stay one level up, at the host's own top level, ahead of this file.

        WHAT THIS FILE FIXES. Before it, every host hand assembled six to eight dictionaries and
        had to know that the semantic status palette must be merged at application level (see the
        note on Status.Light.xaml below). Two of the eight consumers in this repository, both
        sample applications, had silently got that wrong: every status colour fell back to a
        control default, with no crash and no warning, because status keys are consumed with
        DynamicResource and a missing DynamicResource key just leaves the property alone.

        WHAT THIS FILE LEAVES OUT, on purpose:

        - Assets/Shapes/IriShapes.xaml and Assets/Shapes/IranBoundaries.xaml. Iran specific
          geometry; merge them yourself if you draw them.
        - Assets/Styles/Controls.SecurityInputs.xaml. IRI.Maptor.Styles.Security.FieldGroup binds
          to an ancestor named "root", so it only works when merged by a view that has one. The
          Security views merge it themselves.
        - Assets/Styles/MenuIconStyles.xaml, MapOptionStyles.xaml and FeatureTableFilters.xaml.
          The views that need these merge them into their own Resources.
        - Fluent.Ribbon. A separate package that not every host references; ThemeHelper.ApplyTheme
          re themes it at runtime when it is present.-->

    <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>

        <!--Maptor control styles, and the metric, effect and over map brush tokens they use.-->
        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Wpf;component/Assets/Styles/Controls.All.xaml"/>

        <!--Converters, fonts, fixed brand colours, marker animations and the generic shape
            geometries. Most views merge the ones they need into their own Resources as well;
            having them at application level costs nothing and covers the views that do not.-->
        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Wpf;component/Assets/IRI.Maptor.Converters.xaml"/>
        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Wpf;component/Assets/IRI.Maptor.Fonts.xaml"/>
        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Wpf;component/Assets/IRI.Maptor.Colors.xaml"/>
        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Wpf;component/Assets/IRI.Maptor.Animations.xaml"/>
        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Wpf;component/Assets/Shapes/Others.xaml"/>

        <!--Semantic status palette (valid / invalid / warning / muted), light variant.

            It is deliberately NOT inside Controls.All.xaml. Views merge Controls.All into their
            OWN Resources, and WPF resolves a resource by walking the element tree's dictionaries
            before Application.Resources, so a status palette bundled there would always be found
            first and the runtime dark swap could never take effect. See the design system README
            section 4.4c.

            Here it is safe, because this file is merged at application level only.
            ThemeHelper.ApplyTheme appends Status.Light or Status.Dark to
            Application.Resources.MergedDictionaries and a later sibling wins the lookup, so the
            runtime variant shadows this one. ApplyTheme's removal pass only scans the top level of
            that list and will not see this nested entry, which is intended: it is the fallback for
            a host that never calls ApplyTheme at all.

            Consume these keys with DynamicResource, never StaticResource, or the swap will not
            reach you.-->
        <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/IRI.Maptor.Presentation.Wpf;component/Assets/Styles/Status.Light.xaml"/>

    </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>

    <!--Twelve library views bind {StaticResource Localization} without declaring the provider
        themselves, eight of them in the SLD editor. In a host that does not declare it at
        application level those views throw when they load, because a StaticResource that cannot be
        resolved is a hard failure rather than a silent default. Declaring it here means a host
        cannot forget it. LocalizationManager is a singleton, so a host that already declares its
        own Localization key keeps its own: entries declared directly on Application.Resources are
        searched before any merged dictionary.-->
    <ObjectDataProvider x:Key="Localization" ObjectInstance="{x:Static localization:LocalizationManager.Instance}"/>

</ResourceDictionary>
